**Route 1: prepayment of a usance bill.** The buyer pays early and gets a **cash discount**, capped at the **interest on the unexpired period of usance**. Use the rate in the contract, or if none, a prime rate or accepted benchmark in the **invoice currency**.

**Route 2: any other reduction** after the bill has been negotiated or sent for collection. The bank may approve if it is satisfied of genuineness and:
- the cut is **no more than 25%** of the invoice value;
- the goods are **not subject to a floor price**;
- the exporter is **not on RBI's caution list**;
- the exporter is told to **surrender proportionate export incentives**.

**Experienced exporters** (more than three years in business with a satisfactory record, meaning outstanding exports **not above 5%** of average annual realisation over three years) may be allowed a reduction **with no percentage ceiling**, still subject to the other conditions.

**Before sanction**, the bank checks that:
- the borrower is the **bank's customer** with an **IEC**;
- the name is **not on RBI's caution list** or ECGC's **specific approval list**;
- the exporter has a **confirmed order or letter of credit** and can **execute it in time**;
- **know-your-customer** rules are met;
- the **limit fits expected turnover**, and the **period fits the manufacturing cycle**.

**After sanction**:
- **No second packing credit** from another bank for the same order.
- **Stock statements** for the goods financed.
- **Credit reports** on the foreign buyer, and on the **letter of credit** bank.
- **Liquidate** the packing credit from the **export bills**; if **no export takes place**, it becomes a **local advance** at **domestic penal rates**.

RBI lets authorised dealer banks **factor export receivables on a non-recourse basis**. A bank must:

- decide **for itself** whether to enter such an arrangement;
- make sure the client is **not over-financed**;
- check the invoices are **genuine trade invoices**;
- have an **arrangement with an import factor** for credit evaluation and collection (or, for a **single factor** with no overseas partner, get credit details from the **correspondent bank abroad**);
- ensure a **notation on the invoice** that the importer must **pay the import factor**;
- do **KYC and due diligence** on the exporter;
- after factoring, **close the export bills** and **report in EDPMS**.

Separately, **Exim Bank and authorised dealer banks** may undertake **forfaiting**.

**Section 3: dealing.** Unless permitted, **no one may**
- **deal in or transfer foreign exchange** to anyone who is **not an authorised person**;
- **make any payment to or for the credit** of a **non-resident**;
- **receive** a payment for a non-resident **otherwise than through an authorised person**;
- enter into a **financial transaction in India** in return for acquiring an **asset outside India**.

**Section 4: holding.** A **resident of India** may not **acquire, hold, own, possess or transfer** foreign exchange, foreign security or **immovable property outside India**, unless the Act allows.

**Section 5: current account.** **Anyone may sell or draw** foreign exchange through an authorised person for a **current account** transaction. The Government may impose **reasonable restrictions** in the **public interest**.

**Section 6: capital account.** Permissible classes, limits and conditions are **specified by the Reserve Bank and the Government**. A resident may still **keep what he acquired while non-resident or inherited from a non-resident**.

**Promoter pledge for an ECB**: a promoter of a borrowing company that raised an ECB may pledge the company's shares (or those of resident associates) to secure the ECB, if the pledge is **co-terminous with the ECB**, the **statutory auditor certifies the end-use**, and an **authorised dealer gives a no-objection**.

**Non-resident holder's pledge**
- to a **bank in India**, for credit to the **Indian company** for **bona fide purposes**;
- to an **overseas bank**, for credit to the holder, the Indian company's **promoter** or its **overseas group company**;
- to a **registered NBFC**, for credit to the **Indian company**;
- always subject to the **authorised dealer** being satisfied of compliance.

**On invocation**: the transfer follows the **entry route, sectoral caps, pricing guidelines and conditions in force when the pledge was created**.

**Set-off (any exporter/importer)**: an authorised dealer may set off **outstanding export receivables** against **outstanding import payables** with the **same overseas party**, or among **group and associate companies** through a **centralised settlement arrangement**, net or gross, if:

- **one dealer** operates and supervises it;
- the dealer is satisfied on **bona fides, KYC/AML**; invoices are **not under investigation**;
- **ACU** transactions stay **outside**;
- **goods are not set off against services**;
- **both legs are outstanding** and fall in the **same calendar year**;
- a **verifiable agreement** (bilateral) or a **written enforceable contract** (group) supports it; no **tax avoidance**;
- each export and import is **still reported gross** in the monitoring systems, using the **set-off indicator**.

**Netting for SEZ units (narrower)**: only **bilateral** with the **same overseas buyer/supplier**, **as at the balance sheet date**; exports on **EDF/DTR**, imports on **A1/A2**; the export form is **treated as complete only after the entire proceeds are adjusted or received**.

When a foreign buyer **pays in advance**, the exporter must:

- **ship within three years** of receiving it;
- pay interest, if any, of **not more than the benchmark rate + 100 basis points**;
- **route the shipping documents through the dealer that received the advance**.

After **three years**, **no refund or interest can be remitted without RBI approval**.

**Long-term advance (up to 10 years)**, for long supply contracts, needs an exporter with **at least three years' satisfactory record** and conditions such as: **firm irrevocable orders**, a **vetted contract**, **capacity to execute**, a **clean enforcement record**, **adjustment through future exports**, interest **at most benchmark + 200 bp**, **documents through one dealer**, **no use to clear NPA rupee loans**, **no double financing**, and **immediate reporting to RBI of an advance of USD 100 million or more**.

**Credit risk** = a **borrower does not pay interest or instalments** when due or on demand. It also arises:
- on **guarantees and letters of credit** when the liability **crystallises**;
- in **treasury products** when a **counterparty stops paying**;
- in **trading** when **settlement fails**;
- in **cross-border exposure** when **transfer of currency is restricted**.

**Lending is more than half** of a bank's risk-taking and is spread over **many branches**, so the policy must be **communicated across the organisation**.

**Framework**: answer **four questions** (what risks; which and how much to accept; how to monitor and control; how to reduce) through **identification, measurement, monitoring and control, mitigation**.

| Body | Role |
| --- | --- |
| **Board** | **overall responsibility**; **policies, procedures, aggregate limits**, reporting and audit; sets the **level of credit risk** |
| **Risk Management Committee** | **guidelines, conformity, prudential limits, robust models, staffing** |
| **CPC / CRMC** | policy on **credit proposals, covenants, rating standards, delegation, large exposures, concentrations, collateral, portfolio, loan review, pricing, provisioning** |
| **Credit Risk Management Department** | **independent of credit administration**; **enforces limits**, builds **rating and MIS systems**, **loan review** |

**Under the Reserve Bank's 2025 Directions** a bank must:
- have a **comprehensive Board approved credit risk management policy** (related parties, **country risk, unhedged foreign currency exposure**, valuation of properties, current/CC/OD accounts);
- follow the **Guidance Note on Credit Risk Management**;
- do **independent credit appraisal**, **not relying on outside or the borrower's in-house consultants**;
- run **sensitivity tests and scenario analysis for infrastructure projects** (delays, **cost overruns**);
- check the **source and quality of promoters' equity** (**no parent debt infused as subsidiary equity**);
- obtain **credit information reports** from credit information companies.
